"use strict";(self.webpackChunklost_notch=self.webpackChunklost_notch||[]).push([["6607"],{116(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"snyder-brook","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/snyder-brook","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-27-snyder-brook/index.mdx","title":"Snyder Brook","description":"Snyder Brook, in Randolph, runs roughly parallel to Valley Way. The Fallsway and Brookbank trails hug the edges of Snyder Brook and feature views of many waterfalls, making either trail a great alternative to the lower section of Valley Way.","date":"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Northern Presidentials","permalink":"/blog/tags/northern-presidentials","description":"The northern half of the Presidential Range"}],"readingTime":0.41,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"snyder-brook","title":"Snyder Brook","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","northern-presidentials"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Church Pond","permalink":"/blog/church-pond"}},"content":"Snyder Brook, in Randolph, runs roughly parallel to Valley Way. The Fallsway and Brookbank trails hug the edges of Snyder Brook and feature views of many waterfalls, making either trail a great alternative to the lower section of Valley Way.\\n\\nHere\'s Tama Fall, just off Brookbank.\\n\\n![Tama Fall](./tama.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nUpper Salroc Falls.\\n\\n![Upper Salroc](./upper-salroc.jpg)\\n\\nGordon Fall. \\n\\n![Gordon Fall](./gordon.jpg)\\n\\nAnd these are just the waterfalls with names. There are many unnamed rills and falls along Snyder Brook too."},{"id":"church-pond","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/church-pond","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-19-church-pond/index.mdx","title":"Church Pond","description":"Church Pond is a little more than a mile off the Kancamagus Highway, but it feels much more remote. The usual route to the pond is from Camp Passaconaway via the Church Pond Loop Trail. While this used to be a loop, the east section of the trail is permanently closed, now making it an out-and-back route.","date":"2026-10-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Carrigain Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/carrigain-region","description":"The region around Mt. Carrigain and the Moats"}],"readingTime":1.01,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"church-pond","title":"Church Pond","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","carrigain-region"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Snyder Brook","permalink":"/blog/snyder-brook"},"nextItem":{"title":"Zealand Notch","permalink":"/blog/zealand-notch"}},"content":"Church Pond is a little more than a mile off the Kancamagus Highway, but it feels much more remote. The usual route to the pond is from Camp Passaconaway via the Church Pond Loop Trail. While this used to be a loop, the east section of the trail is permanently closed, now making it an out-and-back route.\\n\\nAfter walking over bog bridges through a beautiful marsh, you arrive at a knoll on the east side of the pond. Follow one of the herd paths down to the shore for some great views especially to the southwest. Here is a dramatic view of Mt. Potash (left) rising on the other side of the Kancamagus Highway. To the right of Mt. Potash is the valley of Sabbaday Brook with the Sleepers in the distance.\\n\\n![Mt. Potash](./potash.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nSwing to the right just a little and North Tripyramid comes into view.\\n\\n![North Tripyramid](./tripyramid.jpg)\\n\\nAlthough it\'s definitely past peak foliage, the tamaracks are almost neon yellow this time of year. \\n\\n![Tamaracks](./tamaracks.jpg)\\n\\nTamaracks are common in New Hampshire, but they almost never dominate the landscape. It\'s always a pleasant surprise to catch the tamarack show after most hardwoods have dropped their leaves."},{"id":"zealand-notch","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/zealand-notch","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-30-zealand-notch/index.mdx","title":"Zealand Notch","description":"Low clouds hanging over Zealand Notch make for vibrant early fall foliage. Here is a view across Zealand Pond toward Mt. Hale.","date":"2026-09-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Franconia Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/franconia-region","description":"Franconia Notch and the Pemigewasset Wilderness"},{"inline":false,"label":"Appalachian Trail","permalink":"/blog/tags/appalachian-trail","description":"New Hampshire locations on the Appalachian Trail"}],"readingTime":0.32,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"zealand-notch","title":"Zealand Notch","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","franconia-region","appalachian-trail"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Church Pond","permalink":"/blog/church-pond"},"nextItem":{"title":"Guinea Pond","permalink":"/blog/guinea-pond"}},"content":"Low clouds hanging over Zealand Notch make for vibrant early fall foliage. Here is a view across Zealand Pond toward Mt. Hale. \\n\\n![Black Mountain](./zealand-pond.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nAnother view of the pond further up Zealand Trail.\\n\\n![Sandwich Dome](./zealand-pond-again.jpg)\\n\\nThe colors are especially brilliant around Zealand Falls Hut. This is from the top of Zealand Falls.\\n\\n![sandwich-mountain](./top-of-falls.jpg)"},{"id":"guinea-pond","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/guinea-pond","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-22-guinea-pond/index.mdx","title":"Guinea Pond","description":"Guinea Pond is located in the Sandwich Range Wilderness, just south of Sandwich Mountain. Black Mountain, a subpeak of Sandwich Mountain, looms over the pond.","date":"2026-09-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Waterville Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/waterville-region","description":"The region around Waterville Valley and east to Mt. Whiteface"}],"readingTime":0.42,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"guinea-pond","title":"Guinea Pond","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","waterville-region"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Zealand Notch","permalink":"/blog/zealand-notch"},"nextItem":{"title":"Mt. Success","permalink":"/blog/mt-success"}},"content":"Guinea Pond is located in the Sandwich Range Wilderness, just south of Sandwich Mountain. Black Mountain, a subpeak of Sandwich Mountain, looms over the pond. \\n\\n![Black Mountain](./black-mountain.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nTo the right of Black Mountain is Sandwich Dome, the true summit of Sandwich Mountain.\\n\\n![Sandwich Dome](./sandwich-dome.jpg)\\n\\nZooming out a bit, you can see how truly massive Sandwich Mountain is. From this angle, it looks like an entire mountain range, but geographically speaking, it is a single mountain.\\n\\n![sandwich-mountain](./sandwich-mountain.jpg)"},{"id":"mt-success","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/mt-success","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-15-mt-success/index.mdx","title":"Mt. Success","description":"Imagine you are hiking the Appalachian Trail northbound. You are almost finished with New Hampshire. You just have one more climb -- Mt. Success. As you near the summit, you look south to see some of the mountains you have already traveled over, the Carter-Moriah Range and the Northern Presidentials.","date":"2026-09-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Mahoosuc Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/mahoosuc-region","description":"The region around the Mahoosuc Range"},{"inline":false,"label":"Appalachian Trail","permalink":"/blog/tags/appalachian-trail","description":"New Hampshire locations on the Appalachian Trail"}],"readingTime":0.45,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mt-success","title":"Mt. Success","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","mahoosuc-region","appalachian-trail"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Guinea Pond","permalink":"/blog/guinea-pond"},"nextItem":{"title":"Monadnock Summit Bog","permalink":"/blog/monadnock-summit-bog"}},"content":"Imagine you are hiking the Appalachian Trail northbound. You are almost finished with New Hampshire. You just have one more climb -- Mt. Success. As you near the summit, you look south to see some of the mountains you have already traveled over, the Carter-Moriah Range and the Northern Presidentials.\\n\\n![Looking south](./south.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nYou push north over bog bridges to the summit of Mt. Success.\\n\\n![Bog bridges](./bog-bridges.jpg)\\n\\nFrom there, you get your first proper look at Goose Eye and the mountains of Maine. \\n\\n![Looking north](./maine.jpg)"},{"id":"monadnock-summit-bog","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/monadnock-summit-bog","source":"@site/blog/2026-06-30-monadnock-summit-bog/index.mdx","title":"Monadnock Summit Bog","description":"There are many tiny microclimates scattered around the summit of Mt. Monadnock. It\'s fascinating to return to favorite spots at different times of the year. This bog is just below the summit on Pumpelly Trail. Today, the bog is full of spring green foliage.","date":"2026-06-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Mt. Monadnock","permalink":"/blog/tags/monadnock","description":"Mt. Monadnock"}],"readingTime":0.6,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"monadnock-summit-bog","title":"Monadnock Summit Bog","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["monadnock"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Mt. Success","permalink":"/blog/mt-success"},"nextItem":{"title":"Chocorua in the Clouds","permalink":"/blog/chocorua-in-clouds"}},"content":"There are many tiny microclimates scattered around the summit of Mt. Monadnock. It\'s fascinating to return to favorite spots at different times of the year. This bog is just below the summit on Pumpelly Trail. Today, the bog is full of spring green foliage.\\n\\n<div class=\\"text--center\\">\\n\\n![Spring](./01.jpg)\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nLater this summer, the bog cotton will emerge to dance in the wind (photo from August, 2024).\\n\\n<div class=\\"text--center\\">\\n\\n![Summer](./03.jpg)\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nAutumn brings deep, rusty tones (photo from October, 2023). \\n\\n<div class=\\"text--center\\">\\n\\n![Fall](./05.jpg)\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nAnd the long winter above 3000 feet is almost entirely black and white (photo from November, 2025). \\n\\n<div class=\\"text--center\\">\\n\\n![Fall](./07.jpg)\\n\\n</div>"},{"id":"chocorua-in-clouds","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/chocorua-in-clouds","source":"@site/blog/2026-04-15-chocorua-in-clouds/index.mdx","title":"Chocorua in the Clouds","description":"The summit of Mt. Chocorua is often crowded with hikers. Apparently, unsettled weather keeps the crowds at bay. It is a rare treat to have the summit to yourself.","date":"2026-04-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Chocorua Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/chocorua-region","description":"The region around Mt. Chocorua and west to Mt. Whiteface"}],"readingTime":0.33,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"chocorua-in-clouds","title":"Chocorua in the Clouds","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","chocorua-region"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Monadnock Summit Bog","permalink":"/blog/monadnock-summit-bog"},"nextItem":{"title":"Castle Ravine","permalink":"/blog/castle-ravine"}},"content":"The summit of Mt. Chocorua is often crowded with hikers. Apparently, unsettled weather keeps the crowds at bay. It is a rare treat to have the summit to yourself.\\n\\n![Looking northeast](./northeast.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nEven though the clouds cover most neighboring peaks, North Tripyramid might make an appearance.\\n\\n![North Tripyramid](./north-tri.jpg)\\n\\nAnd it is magical walking along the exposed section of Piper Trail.\\n\\n![Piper Trail](./piper.jpg)"},{"id":"castle-ravine","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/castle-ravine","source":"@site/blog/2025-08-15-castle-ravine/index.mdx","title":"Castle Ravine","description":"Castle Ravine is a glacial cirque on the north side of Mt. Jefferson. Here\'s a view down the ravine from Castle Ravine Trail.","date":"2025-08-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Northern Presidentials","permalink":"/blog/tags/northern-presidentials","description":"The northern half of the Presidential Range"}],"readingTime":0.51,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"castle-ravine","title":"Castle Ravine","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","northern-presidentials"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Chocorua in the Clouds","permalink":"/blog/chocorua-in-clouds"},"nextItem":{"title":"North of Lafayette","permalink":"/blog/north-of-lafayette"}},"content":"Castle Ravine is a glacial cirque on the north side of Mt. Jefferson. Here\'s a view down the ravine from Castle Ravine Trail.\\n\\n![Castle Ravine](./01.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nOn the west side of the ravine is Castellated Ridge with it\'s dramatic rock formations or \\"castles\\". That\'s Cherry Mountain just beyond the lower castle.\\n\\n![Castles](./02.jpg)\\n\\nAnd on the east side of the ravine is Israel Ridge. \\n\\n![Israel Ridge](./03.jpg)\\n\\nThe main trails around the ravine are Castle Trail, Castle Ravine Trail, and Israel Ridge Trail. You can combine the main trails with various connecting trails to make many interesting routes."},{"id":"north-of-lafayette","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/north-of-lafayette","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-25-north-of-lafayette/index.mdx","title":"North of Lafayette","description":"The Franconia Ridge is very popular -- especially between Little Haystack and Mt. Lafayette. Day hikers are less inclined to venture north of Mt. Lafayette, but it\'s just as beautiful. This is Garfield Ridge Trail just north of Lafayette.","date":"2025-06-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Franconia Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/franconia-region","description":"Franconia Notch and the Pemigewasset Wilderness"},{"inline":false,"label":"Appalachian Trail","permalink":"/blog/tags/appalachian-trail","description":"New Hampshire locations on the Appalachian Trail"}],"readingTime":0.37,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"north-of-lafayette","title":"North of Lafayette","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","franconia-region","appalachian-trail"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Castle Ravine","permalink":"/blog/castle-ravine"},"nextItem":{"title":"Alpine Garden","permalink":"/blog/alpine-garden"}},"content":"The Franconia Ridge is very popular -- especially between Little Haystack and Mt. Lafayette. Day hikers are less inclined to venture north of Mt. Lafayette, but it\'s just as beautiful. This is Garfield Ridge Trail just north of Lafayette.\\n\\n![Looking northeast](./01.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nFurther north, Mt. Garfield comes into view.\\n\\n![North Tripyramid](./03.jpg)\\n\\nAnd here\'s a closer look at Garfield with some Labrador Tea blooming in the foreground.\\n\\n![Piper Trail](./05.jpg)"},{"id":"alpine-garden","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/alpine-garden","source":"@site/blog/2025-06-09-alpine-garden/index.mdx","title":"Alpine Garden","description":"The Alpine Garden is an area above treeline on Mt. Washington\'s eastern slope between Tuckerman Ravine and Huntington Ravine. It\'s a place where several varieties of alpine plants produce flowers in June and into July. June 9 is early for the flowers of some varieties, but Lapland Rosebay (pink) and Diapensia (white) are blooming on the edge of Tuckerman Ravine.","date":"2025-06-09T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Washington Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/washington-region","description":"Mt. Washington and the southern ridges"}],"readingTime":0.66,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"alpine-garden","title":"Alpine Garden","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","washington-region"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"North of Lafayette","permalink":"/blog/north-of-lafayette"},"nextItem":{"title":"Mountain View Farm","permalink":"/blog/mountain-view-farm"}},"content":"The Alpine Garden is an area above treeline on Mt. Washington\'s eastern slope between Tuckerman Ravine and Huntington Ravine. It\'s a place where several varieties of alpine plants produce flowers in June and into July. June 9 is early for the flowers of some varieties, but Lapland Rosebay (pink) and Diapensia (white) are blooming on the edge of Tuckerman Ravine.\\n\\n![The edge of Tuckerman Ravine](./edge-of-tuckerman.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThe high winds of the Alpine Garden cause the stunted trees to twist into strange shapes or Krummholz.\\n\\n![Krummholz](./krummholz.jpg)\\n\\nDespite the difficult conditions, Alpine Azalea is thriving. This plant is in a little nook on the leeward side of a small rock. \\n\\n![Alpine Azalea](./alpine-azalea.jpg)\\n\\nAnd there are large patches of Diapensia and Lapland Rosebay everywhere you look. \\n\\n![Alpine Garden](./garden.jpg)"},{"id":"mountain-view-farm","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/mountain-view-farm","source":"@site/blog/2024-07-22-mountain-view-farm/index.mdx","title":"Mountain View Farm","description":"The Daniel Doan Trail is a little-used route to the summit of Smarts Mountain. The trailhead is close to Mountain View Farm in Quinttown, a section of Orford, New Hampshire.","date":"2024-07-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Moosilauke Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/moosilauke-region","description":"Mt. Moosilauke and west to the Vermont border"},{"inline":false,"label":"Appalachian Trail","permalink":"/blog/tags/appalachian-trail","description":"New Hampshire locations on the Appalachian Trail"}],"readingTime":0.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mountain-view-farm","title":"Mountain View Farm","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["moosilauke-region","appalachian-trail"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Alpine Garden","permalink":"/blog/alpine-garden"},"nextItem":{"title":"Moosilauke South Peak","permalink":"/blog/moosilauke-south-peak"}},"content":"The Daniel Doan Trail is a little-used route to the summit of Smarts Mountain. The trailhead is close to [Mountain View Farm](https://uvlt.org/conservation-areas/mountain-view-farm/) in Quinttown, a section of Orford, New Hampshire.\\n\\n![Old Shack](./shack.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThe fields of Mountain View Farm offer views of the surrounding hills including Mt. Cube.\\n\\n![Barn with Mt. Cube in the background](./barn-wide.jpg)\\n\\nSmarts Mountain is on the 52 with a View (NH52WAV) list, but most hikers take a different route to the summit. It\'s a shame. If more people knew about Mountain View Farm, the Daniel Doan Trail might be more popular. \\n\\n<div class=\\"text--center\\">\\n\\n![Barn again](./barn.jpg)\\n\\n</div>"},{"id":"moosilauke-south-peak","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/moosilauke-south-peak","source":"@site/blog/2024-06-18-moosilauke-south-peak/index.mdx","title":"Moosilauke South Peak","description":"Mt. Moosilauke is massive. In addition to the main summit commonly sought by NH48 peak baggers, it has three subpeaks over 4000 feet: Mt. Jim, Mt. Blue, and South Peak. These subpeaks are sometimes overlooked because they lack the prominence required by the NH48 list, but South Peak in particular has some great views.","date":"2024-06-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Moosilauke Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/moosilauke-region","description":"Mt. Moosilauke and west to the Vermont border"}],"readingTime":0.67,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"moosilauke-south-peak","title":"Moosilauke South Peak","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","moosilauke-region"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Mountain View Farm","permalink":"/blog/mountain-view-farm"},"nextItem":{"title":"Mt. Crawford","permalink":"/blog/mt-crawford"}},"content":"Mt. Moosilauke is massive. In addition to the main summit commonly sought by [NH48](https://4000footers.com/nh.shtml) peak baggers, it has three subpeaks over 4000 feet: Mt. Jim, Mt. Blue, and South Peak. These subpeaks are sometimes overlooked because they lack the prominence required by the NH48 list, but South Peak in particular has some great views.\\n\\nYou reach South Peak by a short spur at the junction of Glencliff Trail and Moosilauke Carriage Road. Here\'s a view looking northwest from South Peak. That\'s Black Mountain just right of center on the horizon.\\n\\n![Northwest](./black-mountain.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nSwing to the northeast and the main summit of Moosilauke comes into view.\\n\\n![Northeast](./moosilauke.jpg)\\n\\nBack to the northwest, here\'s a view of the Tunnel Brook valley between Moosilauke and Mt. Clough. \\n\\n![Tunnel Brook](./tunnel-brook.jpg)"},{"id":"mt-crawford","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/mt-crawford","source":"@site/blog/2023-10-24-mt-crawford/index.mdx","title":"Mt. Crawford","description":"It\'s just over 3100 feet high, but Mt. Crawford has some of the best views in the White Mountains. Crawford Notch is just to the northwest.","date":"2023-10-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"White Mountains","permalink":"/blog/tags/white-mountains","description":"The White Mountains of New Hampshire"},{"inline":false,"label":"Washington Region","permalink":"/blog/tags/washington-region","description":"Mt. Washington and the southern ridges"}],"readingTime":0.38,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mt-crawford","title":"Mt. Crawford","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["white-mountains","washington-region"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Moosilauke South Peak","permalink":"/blog/moosilauke-south-peak"},"nextItem":{"title":"Monadnock after a Nor\'easter","permalink":"/blog/monadnock-under-snow"}},"content":"It\'s just over 3100 feet high, but Mt. Crawford has some of the best views in the White Mountains. Crawford Notch is just to the northwest.\\n\\n![Crawford Notch](./crawford-notch.jpg)\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nStairs Mountain is further up Davis Path to the northeast.\\n\\n![Stairs Mountain](./stairs.jpg)\\n\\nMt. Washington is almost due north. \\n\\n![Mt. Washington](./washington.jpg)\\n\\nAnd that\'s just 90 degrees of the compass. Mt. Crawford has close to 360 degrees of views. It\'s well worth a visit."},{"id":"monadnock-under-snow","metadata":{"permalink":"/blog/monadnock-under-snow","source":"@site/blog/2023-03-16-monadnock-under-snow/index.mdx","title":"Monadnock after a Nor\'easter","description":"Yesterday, a late season nor\'easter dumped more than two feet of snow on the Monadnock region. The entire summit of Monadnock is under a blanket of white. If you look closely, you can see a lone hiker near the top of the mountain.","date":"2023-03-16T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Mt. Monadnock","permalink":"/blog/tags/monadnock","description":"Mt. Monadnock"}],"readingTime":0.48,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Lost Notch Caretaker","title":"Hiker, Trail Adopter, Photographer, Writer","url":"/caretaker","imageURL":"/img/caretaker.png","key":"caretaker","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"monadnock-under-snow","title":"Monadnock after a Nor\'easter","authors":["caretaker"],"tags":["monadnock"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Mt. Crawford","permalink":"/blog/mt-crawford"}},"content":"Yesterday, a late season nor\'easter dumped more than two feet of snow on the Monadnock region. The entire summit of Monadnock is under a blanket of white. If you look closely, you can see a lone hiker near the top of the mountain.\\n\\n<div class=\\"text--center\\">\\n\\n![Monadnock summit](./summit.jpg)\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\nThe high winds sculpted dramatic shapes in the snow.\\n\\n![Wind sculpted snow](./sculpted-snow.jpg)\\n\\nThe underlying rock emerges in places to remind us the snow is temporary. Spring is just around the corner. The melting snow will help the alpine plants thrive. \\n\\n![Detail](./detail.jpg)"}]}}')}}]);